WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:08.029
Two or three days ago, I got a letter from
one of our fans and it’s different from

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:15.309
any other letter that we have ever had, because
this fan, Young, has attached to it a sample

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:21.220
of an element and what’s more it is an element
that I had never seen.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:27.970
It’s scandium. It’s only a small piece
but according to my colleague Steve, it’s

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:36.850
a very expensive element, so this might be
quite valuable, and scandium is a really interesting

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:44.420
element, much more interesting than I realised,
when I first talked about it on the video.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.820
Scandium is an element which chemists usually
ignore, I have never used scandium in any

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:50.820
of my research.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:57.600
It was one of the elements that Mendeleev
predicted ought to exist when he first devised

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.920
the periodic table, when he made it there
were lots of elements which were not known,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.750
but what he did was to predict that there
were holes in various places where he felt

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.300
that there ought to be an element and because
obviously these elements did not have names,

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:24.490
he called them Ekea boron or Ekea aluminium
or Ekea silicon meaning underneath those elements.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:32.360
And the triumph of the periodic table was
that people actually discovered these elements.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:40.410
And the first one that was discovered was
the element Gallium (Ekea Aluminium) and the

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:47.680
second one was Ekea Boron which is scandium.
Now you might think that it is a bit surprising

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.800
because if you look at a modern periodic table,
scandium and boron are really quite a long

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:00.140
way apart but in the original way that Medeleev
started the periodic table when he wrote it

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:07.950
down first, scandium and boron came very close
together because both of them, have three

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.810
electrons in their outer shell which makes
their chemistry quite similar. Of course Medeleev

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:22.549
did not know about electrons, but he guessed
from looking at titanium which has four electrons

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:28.719
and calcium which has two that he thought
there was something missing and what is also

00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:34.349
interesting about these three elements that
he predicted is that they all ended up with

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:40.980
names of countries. There was gallium that
was named after France, from the latin word

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:47.519
for France. Scandium that was named after,
well perhaps not a country but a region, Scandinavia,

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:51.950
and then germanium, that was named after Germany.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:57.819
The first time that an element was discovered,
when gallium was discovered, people said well

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:04.430
it was just luck, but when scandium was discovered,
they really began to take Mendeleev pretty

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:11.519
seriously, and by the time germanium was discovered
they really all decided that Mendeleev was

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:20.269
right. Scandium, because it is an uncommon
element, is one where people don’t know

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:26.810
much about the chemistry. I have never really
done any research in this area but like many

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:34.409
elements suddenly there comes a time where
they become really quite popular and topical

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:40.950
and now is the time for scandium and the reason
is that scandium is a really light metal.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:49.799
Its density is slightly less than 4, that’s
4 grams per cubic centimetre and that is very

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:59.189
much lighter than most metals. Copper is nearly
10 and lead and gold are close to 20, so this

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:06.439
is really very light. So why should metals
be important if they are light, and that’s,

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:12.200
the reason is sometimes you want to use a
metal, but you don’t want the object you

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:20.769
make to be very heavy and so for example titanium
which is very light, is used for making everything

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:26.970
from golf clubs to jet fighter planes, where
you want it to be really light either so that

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.930
you can swing it fast or fly fast. For scandium
the reasons are rather different, people are

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:39.360
now thinking about using hydrogen, to power
cars, to use hydrogen gas, to use hydrogen

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:47.660
gas as the fuel to power cars, but what they
want to do is to store the hydrogen, in the

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:54.680
equivalent of the petrol tank. You can’t
just pressurise the hydrogen as in a gas cylinder

00:04:54.680 --> 00:05:00.780
because the container would be so heavy, that
your car would be like a military tank it

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:07.950
would be so heavy. So what they want to try
and do is produce a chemical compound, which

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:14.800
is a bit like a sponge that will absorb the
hydrogen and then for example if you warm

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:21.840
it gently they hydrogen will come out, and
it is here that you want the metal to be light

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.830
because you want this material that you are
using to store hydrogen not to weigh very

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:33.500
much. Because you have got to cart it around
in your car all the time, and scandium compounds

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:43.319
are now looking really quite promising, for
storage of hydrogen, and the idea is that

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:51.020
rather like a molecular model kit, where you
have balls joined with sticks, you can use

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:57.720
organic compounds, those of carbon and hydrogen
with a little oxygen, like sticks to join

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:06.470
together scandium atoms, and produce a structure
which has a lot of holes in it and then the

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:11.800
hydrogen can go into those holes and be stored
without needing an enormous pressure.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.629
Sounds like an incredibly complicated way
to make a fuel tank.

00:06:16.629 --> 00:06:23.650
It is quite complicated but on the other hand,
if it works, you don’t need to understand

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:29.750
the chemistry. You just add the hydrogen to
your tank and away you go.

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:37.490
How can you have got to your ripe old age
as a chemist and never seen scandium?

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:46.229
Well, chemists very often use compounds, salts
of metals, but it’s not too often that you

00:06:46.229 --> 00:06:54.000
use the metal itself and salts because they
don’t have to be purified and so on quite

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:01.330
as much as the metals are easier to get, they
are not as expensive and so there are lots

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:05.160
of the elements which I haven’t seen, but
I am really quite pleased with this sample

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:10.280
of scandium, so when I am finished I am going
to frame it and hang it on my wall.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:16.819
So we have one of my students who works with
one of my colleagues, Martin Schroder and

00:07:16.819 --> 00:07:23.319
the student, has done his whole project, three
year project, on the chemistry of scandium,

00:07:23.319 --> 00:07:29.650
so when I got this letter I asked him: Have
you ever seen scandium? And he hadn’t, he

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:34.150
had never seen the metal, so he was really
quite excited to see John’s letter.

